  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nLow level moisture in proximity to the broad low pressure over\nNova Scotia will continue to produce isolated showers this\nafternoon. This is being helped by daytime instability, and\nthese should dissipate as insolation decreases early this\nevening.\n\nPressure gradient remains tonight, and this will keep a gentle\nbreeze in the region. Did opt to lower overnight lows in most\ncommon radiational cooling locations as these should be more\nsheltered at the surface. This means some mid 40s for the\nnorthern valleys and SW NH. \n\nIt will again be breezy Monday, this time with less moisture and\na drier airmass to mix to the surface. Its possible dewpoints\nwill need to be lowered further, which could result in RH values\nin the mid to upper 30s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nEnsemble guidance continues to place a sub-1000 mb MSLP low \npressure moving thru Quebec north of the Gaspe Peninsula Wed \ninto Thu. Precip will largely be centered around the trailing \ncold front in the form of showers and thunderstorms. The latest \nguidance has trended slower timing-wise and less favorable for \nwidespread thunderstorm activity locally. Ahead of the front \nsouthwest winds will be breezy, owing to a modest LLJ of 40-50 \nknots along the coast. Forecast soundings continue to look \npretty stable throughout the day, so I am not expecting \nsignificant gusts from that LLJ. It will also advect in higher \nmoisture, and dewpoints may be able to climb towards 60 degrees \nacross southern NH. It is possible that this may be enough for \nsome nominal instability across interior zones, enough to \nsupport some thunder threat continuing into the evening. CSU \nmachine learning guidance does continue to show parts of the \nConnecticut River Valley and mtns with a small risk of severe \nthunderstorms, but as the current frontal timing stands I think \nthat is unlikely. \n\nThe precip itself will be showery in nature along the cold \nfront. So I do not anticipate widespread significant rainfall. \nThis is yet another high Pop/low QPF type event, which is more \nor less what the NBM is forecasting at this time.\n\nFinally once the front crosses the area, winds shift to \nnorthwesterly as cold air advection commences. On Thu as mixing \nheights increase that will lead to a gusty day. Forecast \nsoundings over much of the area continue to indicate that gusts \naround 30 mph are likely. However a few spots may push 40 mph \nacross southern NH and coastal western Maine. Once that cooler \nairmass settles into the region, Fri night and Sat night may be \nchilly across many spots. Some widespread 40s are likely, and a \nthe northern valley cold spots may be flirting with upper 30s.",
